[candidate · 3:02] Fair challenge. I think the three buckets are collectively exhaustive if I define bucket three properly — but as I said it, it's sloppy in two places.

First, I lumped "costs" into one bucket without a clean cut. Better to split by P&L line: COGS, store-level operating costs like labor and rent, then corporate SG&A. That way each dollar sits in exactly one place and nothing double-counts — right now labor and rent could arguably sit in either.

Second, I left out mix. If they've shifted toward lower-margin items — say more food, less espresso — or toward lower-margin channels like delivery apps that take a cut, that's a margin driver that isn't strictly revenue growth or cost inflation. It's really a revenue-quality issue, so I'd fold it under revenue as price, volume, mix.

And I'd add store maturity: new stores ramping would drag margin without anything being broken.

So: revenue by price/volume/mix, COGS, store opex, corporate overhead, below-the-line. Does that hold?
