[candidate · 10:58] Let me work this through, rounding as I go.

Start on the old revenue base of 100. Old COGS 42 points: beans 25% of that is about 10.5, other inputs about 31.5.

Since prices haven't moved, all 15% revenue growth is volume — so volumes are up 15% across the board.

Other inputs: 31.5, times 1.15 for volume, times 1.375 for price. 31.5 × 1.15 is roughly 36, times 1.375 is about 50 points.

Now today's actual COGS: 58% of revenue, and revenue is 115, so 0.58 × 115 ≈ 67 points.

So beans today must be about 67 minus 50, call it 17 points. At flat prices beans would be 10.5 × 1.15 ≈ 12. So beans are running roughly 40% above where volume alone explains — bean prices are up around 40%.

Two conclusions: input inflation across the board, beans worst, and zero price pass-through in two years. That's the root cause.
