[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 12:36] Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years.

[candidate · 14:06] Good — 40% matches my back-out, so the gap is fully explained by input inflation, not hidden waste. That's reassuring; it means the fix is commercial, not a store-level cleanup.

Sizing the pricing lever. Prices have been flat for two years while costs inflated. Today COGS is 58% of $480 million, about $280 million. If we pass through a 10% price increase on flat volume, revenue goes to roughly $528 million, and that $48 million drops almost entirely to the bottom line since costs don't move. Profit goes from about $29 million to about $77 million — margin roughly 15%. Rounding, but that's most of the way back.

The risk is elasticity. If a 10% price rise costs us, say, 5% of volume, we lose 5% of revenue but also 5% of that $280 million COGS, about $14 million back — so the net is still strongly positive. Pricing works unless volume falls a long way.

Do we have any elasticity data, or competitor pricing?
