[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 14:08] I don't have elasticity or competitor pricing data. Beyond pricing, what other levers could the client pull to recover margin? Give me a structured set.

[candidate · 17:08] No data, so I'll assume moderate elasticity and flag it as the key test.

Three buckets beyond pricing.

One, procurement. Beans are up 40% and were 25% of COGS — today that's roughly $70 million of spend. Levers: forward contracts or hedging to lock prices, consolidating suppliers for volume discounts, direct-trade sourcing to cut the middleman, and re-specifying blends toward cheaper origins. A 10% procurement win on beans is about $7 million.

Two, mix and product. Push high-margin items — brewed coffee and espresso carry far better margins than dairy-heavy drinks or food. Shifting mix a few points, or upselling sizes, raises effective price without a visible price increase. Also worth testing a lower-cost dairy alternative given dairy sits in that 37.5% inflation bucket.

Three, overhead. It drifted from 12% to 14%, about $10 million on today's revenue. Smaller prize, but it's controllable and doesn't touch the customer.

Labor at 22% has been stable — I'd leave it alone rather than risk service quality.
